  "worked_solution": "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
